  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1020 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern is not expected to change much through\nFriday, with the upper ridge remaining centered over the \nsoutheast US. Occasional disturbances lift from the Four Corners \nregion around the periphery of the ridge, tracking NW of the FA. \nThese provide at least some storm chances Thurs and Fri, primarily\non the Caprock, where upper heights will be slightly lower and \nthe upper disturbance slightly closer (compared to areas off the \nCaprock). While storms may be present each evening, the chance of\nexperiencing rain at any specific location is less than 30%. \nForecast soundings show similar conditions to that described above\nfor Wed evening, thus while organized severe storms are unlikely,\ngusty outflows remain possible with any convection. \n\nOver the course of the weekend, the center of the upper ridge\ngradually shifts west, leading to height rises (subsidence) \nlocally and shifting any potential disturbances further north/west\naway from the FA. Thus, continued above normal temps and mostly \ndry conditions will persist through at least early next week. \n500mb heights are progged to reach the mid/upper 590s. While the \nNBM keeps highs below 105F each day, it would not be a surprise if\nadditional heat advisories are needed at times off the Caprock. \nMedium-to-longer range ensemble guidance suggests this general \npattern will persist into late August, and the CPC outlooks \nindicate a 60-70% chance for above normal temps during the second \nhalf of the month, with the chances for above normal precip \nfocused off to the north and west of the FA.",
